// Let the terminal control WiFi.
//
// The WiFi screen runs nmcli as the deck's own account, and NetworkManager
// asks polkit whether that is allowed. Membership of `netdev` is not the
// answer people expect it to be: the stock policy grants these actions to an
// *active local session* — someone logged in at a seat — and `cyberdeck-ui`
// is a systemd service. It has no seat and no session, so polkit sees a
// subject that is neither active nor inactive and falls through to
// auth_admin. There is no agent to ask, so the call is refused.
//
// The symptom is nmcli's "not authorized to control networking", which says
// nothing about sessions and sends you to check group membership that was
// never the problem. src/wifi.py turns that message into a sentence naming
// this file.
//
// Scoped deliberately narrowly, because the design doc's objection to polkit
// was that a rule is easy to write too broadly:
//
//   * one user, not a group. DECK_USER is substituted at build time.
//   * four action ids, not the org.freedesktop.NetworkManager prefix. Between
//     them they cover exactly what the screen does — switch the radio, scan,
//     join, and save or delete a profile — and nothing else. Notably absent
//     is anything about the system's own connectivity checking or
//     modify.own vs modify.system distinctions beyond what joining needs.
//
// Install: /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/10-cyberdeck-networkmanager.rules, root-owned,
// mode 644. build_deck_image.sh writes it; on a running deck see
// "Updating the code on a running deck" in the runbook.

polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
    var allowed = [
        "org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.enable-disable-wifi",
        "org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.wifi.scan",
        "org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.network-control",
        "org.freedesktop.NetworkManager.settings.modify.system"
    ];
    if (subject.user === "@DECK_USER@" && allowed.indexOf(action.id) !== -1) {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
});
